LAW 3.2 - Storage of sensitive authentication data
Sensitive authentication data must not be retained after authorisation, even encrypted.
This is the requirement most often failed by accident: the data is not stored on purpose,
it is stored in a log, a trace, or a crash dump written by something else.
  ELEMENT the record contains sensitive authentication data
    WHEN /\b(cvv2?|cvc2?|cav2|cid|track[_\s-]?[12]|full[_\s-]?magnetic|pin[_\s-]?block)\b/
  ELEMENT the record is being persisted
    WHEN /\b(insert|update|write|store|save|log|append|dump|persist)\b/
  THEN deny

LAW 3.4 - Rendering the primary account number unreadable
The primary account number must be unreadable anywhere it is stored.
  ELEMENT the record contains a primary account number
    WHEN /\b(pan|card[_\s-]?number|cc[_\s-]?num)\b|\b\d{13,19}\b/
  ELEMENT the record is being persisted
    WHEN /\b(insert|update|write|store|save|persist)\b/
  UNLESS the value is already rendered unreadable
    WHEN /\b(hash|hmac|sha256|tokeni[sz]ed?|truncat|mask|encrypt)\w*\b/
  THEN deny

LAW 8.3 - Multi-factor authentication for administrative access
Administrative access to the cardholder data environment requires more than one factor.
  ELEMENT the access is administrative
    WHEN /\b(admin|root|sudo|superuser|privileged)\b/
  ELEMENT the access reaches the cardholder data environment
    WHEN /(?<![a-z])(cde|cardholder|card[_\s-]?data)(?![a-z])/
  UNLESS a second factor is presented
    WHEN /\b(mfa|2fa|totp|otp|webauthn|yubikey|second[_\s-]?factor)\b/
  THEN require_human

LAW 10.2 - Audit trail
Actions against cardholder data must be recorded in a way the actor cannot alter.
  ELEMENT the action reaches cardholder data
    WHEN /(?<![a-z])(cde|cardholder|card[_\s-]?data|pan)(?![a-z])/
  ELEMENT the action would remove or rewrite the audit trail
    WHEN /(?<![a-z])(truncate|drop|delete|purge|rotate|disable)(?![a-z]).{0,30}(?<![a-z])(audit|log|trail|history)(?![a-z])/
  THEN deny
